# =============================================================================
# Email notifications (SMTP) — kid-friendly setup guide
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS DOES:
#   When the monitor catches a 4xx / 5xx failure, or when a flow fails, or
#   when you click "Snapshot & report" — the backend can send an email to
#   whoever you listed in that project's Settings → Notification emails.
#   (Slack still works the same way via the webhook URL on the project.)
#
# IF YOU SKIP THIS FILE ENTIRELY:
#   The app still runs. Checks keep happening, the dashboard works, Slack
#   still fires. The only thing that breaks is email — every send returns
#   { sent: false, reason: "SMTP not configured" } silently. That's fine
#   for a dev box. Configure it only when you actually want emails.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 1 — Copy this file to .env
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   In a terminal, from the backend/ folder:
#
#       cp .env.example .env       (Mac/Linux)
#       copy .env.example .env     (Windows cmd)
#       Copy-Item .env.example .env   (Windows PowerShell)
#
#   .env is gitignored — your real password will NEVER be committed.
#   Never edit .env.example with real credentials; that file IS committed.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 2 — Get an SMTP password
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   OPTION A — Gmail (easiest for demos, one developer, one Gmail account)
#   -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#     1. Open https://myaccount.google.com → Security
#     2. Turn ON "2-Step Verification" (required — Google won't issue
#        App Passwords without it). Takes 30 seconds.
#     3. After 2-Step is on, open https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
#     4. App name: type "Monitoring Suite" → click "Create"
#     5. Google shows a 16-character password like "abcd efgh ijkl mnop"
#        — copy it WITHOUT the spaces ("abcdefghijklmnop").
#     6. Use it as SMTP_PASS below. (Your regular Gmail password will
#        be rejected — Google only accepts App Passwords from SMTP.)
#
#   OPTION B — Logitech / corporate SMTP (production)
#   --------------------------------------------------
#     Ask IT for: SMTP host, port (usually 587 or 465), username, password,
#     and the FROM address you're allowed to send as. Drop those 4-5 values
#     into the same SMTP_* vars below. No code change needed.
#
#   OPTION C — Other providers (SendGrid, Mailgun, Outlook, AWS SES…)
#   ------------------------------------------------------------------
#     They all give you: host, port, user, password. Same 4 vars work.
#       SendGrid:   SMTP_HOST=smtp.sendgrid.net, SMTP_USER=apikey,
#                   SMTP_PASS=<your SendGrid API key>
#       Outlook:    SMTP_HOST=smtp.office365.com, SMTP_PORT=587
#       AWS SES:    SMTP_HOST=email-smtp.<region>.amazonaws.com,
#                   SMTP_PORT=587, use SES SMTP credentials (not your AWS key)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 3 — Fill in the 5 vars below (in your .env, NOT this file)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   SMTP_HOST   — server address (smtp.gmail.com / smtp.sendgrid.net / …)
#   SMTP_PORT   — usually 587 (STARTTLS) or 465 (SSL). Use 587 for Gmail.
#   SMTP_USER   — login username (usually your full email address)
#   SMTP_PASS   — the App Password / API key from Step 2 (NOT your real password)
#   SMTP_FROM   — the "From:" header recipients will see. Format:
#                 SMTP_FROM="Friendly Name <address@domain.com>"
#                 For Gmail, the address MUST match SMTP_USER.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 4 — Restart the backend so it picks up the new env
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   The backend reads SMTP_* once at startup. After editing .env:
#
#       (Ctrl+C the running `npm run dev`, then start it again)
#
#   tsx watch auto-reloads on .ts file changes but NOT on .env changes.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 5 — Tell each project who should receive emails
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   In the UI: open a project → click the ⚙ cog → "Notification emails"
#   textarea → paste one or more addresses (comma, semicolon, or newline
#   separated). Save. From now on, that project's failure alerts + audit
#   reports also email those addresses.
#
#   Email and Slack are independent — a project can have both, either, or
#   neither. The chip cluster in the project header shows which are on.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STEP 6 — Verify it works
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Quickest test: in the UI, open any project that has Notification
#   emails set, click "📊 Snapshot & report". You should receive an email
#   within ~10 seconds with the HTML report attached. The Audit result
#   modal will also show email: { sent: true } for confirmation.
#
#   If email: { sent: false, reason: "..." } — check the reason:
#     "SMTP not configured"         → SMTP_HOST/PORT/USER/PASS not all set
#     "Invalid login"               → wrong password (Gmail: use App Password!)
#     "self signed certificate"     → corporate SMTP with custom CA — talk to IT
#     "No recipients defined"       → project has no Notification emails set
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY NOTES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   • NEVER paste real passwords into THIS file (.env.example is committed).
#   • .env IS gitignored — safe for real credentials. Double-check with:
#         git check-ignore -v backend/.env
#     (should print the .gitignore line that matches).
#   • Treat App Passwords like passwords — revoke them at
#     https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords if a laptop is lost.
#   • For production, prefer a service account (e.g. monitoring@yourcompany.com)
#     over a personal Gmail — when you leave, the alerts keep flowing.
#
# =============================================================================

SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=your-gmail-address@gmail.com
SMTP_PASS=your-16-char-app-password
SMTP_FROM="Monitoring Suite <your-gmail-address@gmail.com>"
